\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Self-consistency (majority voting over multiple samples) yields large gains on discrete-answer reasoning \citep{wang2023selfconsistency}, and test-time compute scaling has emerged as a complementary axis to model scaling \cite{snell2025scaling, brown2024monkeys}.
But named entity recognition (NER) poses a structurally different challenge: the output is a \textit{set} of entities, two samples may extract overlapping but non-identical sets, and there is no obvious majority to vote on.

Prior work on confidence estimation for LLM-based IE has conflated \textit{quality estimation} (which inputs does the model handle reliably?) with \textit{sample selection} (which of $N$ outputs is best?).
Existing self-consistency methods \cite{wang2023selfconsistency, chen2024usc, taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty} assume outputs can be grouped by equivalence, an assumption that breaks for entity sets with partial overlap.
Unlike \citet{yao2026consistency}, who demonstrate that self-consistency correlates with task accuracy, we investigate \textit{why} this correlation fails to yield selection improvements, diagnosing degeneracy and compression as the two blocking mechanisms.
Our experiments use fine-tuned models, the dominant deployment paradigm for production NER, and separate quality estimation from best-of-$N$ selection.

A methodological premise structures our analysis: four of five signals (SJ, VC, FK, EM) are instance-level aggregates that by design assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates for a given input; only log-probability (LP) is natively per-sample.
These signals achieve reliable quality estimation ($\rho$ up to 0.38 on the primary test bed, 0.42 on Few-NERD; AUROC up to 0.82; Figure~\ref{fig:overview}), supporting selective prediction and error flagging.
Yet quality estimation does not translate to structural selection, a disparity we term the \textbf{correlation-selection gap} (the disparity between a signal's ability to rank instances reliably and its failure to select the best output within a single instance): structural best-of-$N$ selection matches or degrades greedy decoding ($T{=}0$) despite 12\textendash 14~pp oracle headroom.
The gap has two empirical causes: (a)~\textit{instance degeneracy}, where fine-tuned models produce identical outputs for 14\textendash 47\% of instances, eliminating selection headroom despite positive within-instance utility\textendash F1 correlation (median $\rho{=}0.52$); and (b)~\textit{weak LP-quality alignment}, where within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1})$ is too low to exploit the remaining headroom (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
LP selection requires both low degeneracy and adequate alignment: significant at 13.8\% degeneracy with $\rho{\approx}0.5$ ($+1.36$~pp, $p{<}0.001$), sub-significant at 17.8\% with $\rho{\approx}0.24$ ($+0.42$~pp), and absent at 47.4\%.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_overview}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap. \textbf{Left}: consistency signals reliably predict instance-level quality ($\rho$ up to 0.45 on CoNLL, 0.38 on the primary test bed, 0.42 on Few-NERD; AUROC up to 0.82). \textbf{Right}: quality estimation does not translate to selection; all methods approximately match greedy decoding ($T{=}0$) despite 13~pp oracle headroom (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

We evaluate five signals from three families (structural, surface, and model-internal) on SciERC \cite{luan2018scierc} as a primary medium-difficulty test bed, with Few-NERD \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (low-degeneracy contrast), CoNLL-2003 \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (ceiling-effect control), and WNUT-17 \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (signal-collapse boundary), using two 8B models (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B) with a 4B ablation (Qwen3-4B).
We vary candidate pool size ($N{\in}\{2,\ldots,32\}$), signal sophistication, and model capacity within the 4B\textendash 8B range; behavior at 70B+ scales remains an open question.
An exploratory extension to relation extraction is reported in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.
Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Quality estimation framework.}
    We systematically evaluate five confidence signals from three families (structural, surface, model-internal) for LLM-based NER across four benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient, two model families, and a 4B scale ablation.
    Structural signals reliably predict output quality (VC $\rho{=}0.38$ on the primary test bed; EM AUROC${=}0.82$ on CoNLL), with difficulty-dependent but model-stable rankings that support selective prediction and error flagging.

    \item \textbf{Diagnosis of the correlation-selection gap.}
    We identify a three-layer failure:
    (a)~four instance-level signals cannot rank candidates within an instance \textit{by construction};
    (b)~MBR selection with well-aligned utility (within-instance $\rho{=}0.52$) fails because instance degeneracy (14\textendash 47\% of instances yield identical outputs) collapses the candidate pool;
    (c)~LP, the sole per-sample signal, requires two jointly necessary conditions: low degeneracy and adequate LP-quality alignment ($\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}) \gtrsim 0.3$).
    A diagnostic chain (LoRA-rank ablation, free-form decoding, entity-token LP, supervised signal combination) confirms the bottleneck lies in the signal space accessible to unsupervised aggregation, not signal combination methods.

    \item \textbf{Two necessary conditions for LP selection.}
    LP selection requires both low degeneracy and adequate LP-quality alignment: the cross-dataset gradient (47\% $\to$ fail, 18\% $\to$ $+0.42$~pp, 14\% $\to$ $+1.36$~pp at $p{<}0.001$) identifies degeneracy as one condition; a LoRA-rank ablation isolates alignment as the other (degeneracy 7.2\%, LP still fails because $\rho{\approx}0.24$).
    Entity-type-level analysis provides statistical grounding ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$, $n{=}8$); in the low-degeneracy regime, LP selection scales with $N$ ($+0.52$~pp from $N{=}8$ to $N{=}16$, paired $p{=}0.003$).
\end{enumerate}

